<html>
<title>Hot Code Spots</title>
<body>


<h1>Hot Code Spots</h1>

<p>
On the left of the scrollbar a number is shown per executed instruction (2). This number indicates the number of times that specific instruction is executed. Any value larger than 1 is shown in a colour ranging from green to red (1), with green indicating a lower number of executions and red a higher number. The colour is calculated using a logarithmic scale.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Any <font color="#EA6F32">orange</font> text indicates a branch location, either as part of a branch instruction or as the target of a branch.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Any <font color="#b251a7">pink</font> text indicates that a <tt>SEP Rx</tt> instruction ended up at that address.
</p>

<p>
The <strong>Profiler</strong> interface is shown below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/profiler_hot_code.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) Colour range side</li>
<li>(2) Execution counter side</li>
</ul>

<p>
On the <strong>Profiler</strong> tab only a small number of locations are shown at any one time. To scan through memory more quickly for hot code spots, the profiler on the <strong>Memory Dump</strong> tab can be used:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/profiler_memory_dump.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Memory Dump</strong> tab</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Memory</strong> selector, set to <tt>Profiler</tt></li>
<li>(3) Counter values display</li>
<li>(4) <strong>Page navigation</strong> spin buttons</li>
<li>(5) <strong>Address</strong> field</li>
</ul>

<p>
To select the Memory Dump profiler, select the <strong>Memory Dump</strong> tab (1) and set the <strong>Memory</strong> selector (2) to <tt>Profiler</tt>. A total of 256 addresses are shown (3), each containing one counter value between 1 and 255. The colour scheme is the same as on the <strong>Profiler</strong> tab. Instructions executed more frequently are shown in a deeper red colour. The colour is calculated using a logarithmic scale.
</p>

<p>
To change to the previous or next page, use the <strong>Page navigation</strong> spin buttons (4). Alternatively, specify a specific address in the <strong>Address</strong> field (5).
</p>

<p>
For details about the <strong>Memory Dump</strong> tab, see the <a href="DumpMain.htm">Memory Dump</a> section.
</p>
</body>
</html>
